<sect>closestpair (Revision 1.3 -- 27/05/1998)

<p> The unit <tt/closestpair/ provides routines to find a closest pair
in a set of points.

<sect1> Data Structures
<p>
<sect2>coord_2d
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
typedef struct { int pnr; double x; double y; } coord_2d;
</verb></tscreen>
<p>
<p><tt/int pnr/. 
Point number.
<p><tt/double x/.
X-Coordinate of point.
<p><tt/double y/.
Y-Coordinate of point.

<sect1> Routines
<p>
<sect2>clop
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
double clop (coord_2d *yqueue, int n, double lowlim,
             coord_2d *chigh, coord_2d *clow)
</verb></tscreen>
<p> 
This routine attempts to find that pair of points
(out of a coordinate list), whose distance between
each other is minimal, not accepting those smaller
than a lower limit (<tt/lowlim/). It returns the points
in <tt/chigh/ and <tt/clow/. The function's return value is
the found smallest distance or 99999 standing for
infinity.


<sect2>countinbox
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int countinbox(coord_2d *plist, int inlist, int n, int boxsize,
               coord_2d hook, coord_2d *found, FILE *fplog)
</verb></tscreen>
<p>
Given a set of point coordinates and one of those as
<tt/hook/, this procedure looks for 4 other points in
in a quadratic box of size <tt/boxsize/. Look at the
if sequence to see where this box is in relation to
hook.


